WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:10.500
Feature flags and remote configuration are useful because they let teams roll out features, respond to incidents, and change behavior without redeploying code.

2
00:00:10.600 --> 00:00:13.800
That same power can create hidden security states.

3
00:00:13.900 --> 00:00:27.020
A flag that changes authentication behavior, authorization rules, admin access, logging, payment flow, exports, file access, or validation is not just a product toggle.

4
00:00:27.120 --> 00:00:30.240
It is security-relevant configuration.

5
00:00:30.520 --> 00:00:33.240
Dynamic configuration needs governance.

6
00:00:33.340 --> 00:00:42.940
Important flags should have clear names, owners, documentation, approval expectations, target environments, expiration dates, and rollback behavior.

7
00:00:43.040 --> 00:00:50.560
Long-lived stale flags make security behavior hard to reason about because code may contain multiple possible paths controlled outside the repository.

8
00:00:50.660 --> 00:01:01.140
Emergency changes are sometimes necessary, but they should still leave evidence of who changed what, when, why, and how the change was reversed or made permanent.

9
00:01:01.420 --> 00:01:07.660
Remote configuration systems require access control, audit logging, environment separation, and safe defaults.

10
00:01:07.760 --> 00:01:10.800
Runtime changes should be observable and reversible.

11
00:01:10.900 --> 00:01:16.260
If a flag weakens a protection, it should be visible to operations and security teams.

12
00:01:16.360 --> 00:01:19.880
The tradeoff is not whether feature flags are good or bad.

13
00:01:19.980 --> 00:01:27.740
The tradeoff is whether the organization treats them as controlled changes to production behavior rather than invisible switches outside normal review.
